-- title:
--   An automorphism fixing the components of Z(s) fixes Z(s)
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme which is integral and locally Noetherian and whose stalks are all domains and integrally closed (this last pair of conditions being given as a hypothesis $hX$ asserting, for every point $x$ of $X$, that $\mathcal O_{X,x}$ is a domain and is integrally closed). Let $M$ be an $X$-module satisfying `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible`, i.e. every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ such that the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of modules on $U$, and let $s$ be a non-zero morphism from the monoidal unit $\mathbf 1_{X\text{-Mod}}$ to $M$. Write $\mathcal I =$ `Scheme.Modules.zeroSchemeIdeal s` for the smallest ideal sheaf datum $J$ on $X$ with the property that on every affine open $U$ the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ generated by the coefficients of $s$ on $U$ is contained in $J$'s ideal at $U$. Let $\sigma : X \cong X$ be an isomorphism of schemes such that for every subset $C \subseteq X$ which is maximal among the irreducible subsets of $X$ contained in the support of $\mathcal I$, the image of $C$ under the underlying continuous map of $\sigma$ is $C$ itself. Then the pullback (comap) of $\mathcal I$ along $\sigma$ equals $\mathcal I$.
--
--   This is the statement that on a normal integral scheme the closed subscheme cut out by a section of an invertible module is determined by its behaviour in codimension one: an automorphism preserving each irreducible component of the zero locus preserves the zero scheme itself. It feeds the construction of an isomorphism between $M$ and its pullback along $\sigma$ in [`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible.nonempty_pullback_iso_of_forall_maximal_isIrreducible_image_eq`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible.nonempty_pullback_iso_of_forall_maximal_isIrreducible_image_eq).
